"""
============================================================================
ETL 17 - iSDAsoil: SOIL pH + TEXTURE (soils.ph, soils.texture) at 30 m
Land Intelligence Platform - Geocode Spatial Solutions Ltd

What this script does:
  Pulls two soil layers for Kenya from iSDAsoil and catalogues them:
    - soils.ph      : soil pH (H2O), 0-20 cm depth
    - soils.texture : USDA texture class, 0-20 cm (1=Clay ... 12=Sand)
  Both are 30 m, CC-BY-4.0 (commercial use OK with attribution).
  Source: iSDA Africa (Hengl et al. 2021).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERSION 2 - THE FETCH-THEN-REPROJECT REWRITE (session 5)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 1 tried to do everything in one move: open the Africa-wide file over
the internet, reproject it to lon/lat on the fly, and read Kenya's window out
of the reprojected view. That worked in theory and was miserable in practice
(hours, constant truncated-tile errors, sometimes a dead stall).

WHY it was slow, in plain language:
  The file on the server is stored in Web Mercator. We were asking for it in
  lon/lat. Those two grids do not line up, so to hand us one tidy row of
  lon/lat pixels GDAL had to go and collect scraps of source pixels from many
  places at once. Every read turned into thousands of small, scattered
  requests. On a strong connection you barely notice. On a weak one, each
  scattered request is another chance to fail, and the failures compound.
  Note that plain sequential downloading on this same connection is fine
  (etl_13 pulled 96 DEM tiles without complaint). So the bytes were never the
  problem. The reprojection was.

THE FIX - do one thing at a time:
  STAGE A - FETCH. Read Kenya's window in the file's OWN grid (EPSG:3857),
            with no reprojection at all. Because we are not bending the grid,
            the pixels we want sit next to each other in the file, so this
            behaves like a plain sequential download: fast and reliable.
            Save it to a local file.
  STAGE B - REPROJECT. Reproject that small LOCAL file to EPSG:4326 and apply
            the value transform in the same pass. No network involved, so it
            cannot drop a tile. This is just CPU and disk.
  STAGE C - Encode, convert to COG, catalogue in the database.

  Both stages are resumable: each keeps a tiny progress marker file recording
  how many rows are finished, so a dropped connection or a closed laptop costs
  you the last strip, not the whole run.

  This same fetch-then-reproject pattern should be used for EVERY future
  remote COG source. It is the general lesson, not an iSDA quirk.

Why we cannot just download the whole file:
  The iSDA Africa mosaic is 328,563 x 289,306 pixels. A windowed read in the
  native grid is the right unit of work.

Why soil matters for the product:
  pH tells a farmer or buyer whether the land is acidic or alkaline (crop
  suitability, lime cost). Texture (especially heavy CLAY, i.e. "black cotton"
  soil) is a major BUILDING signal: expansive clay cracks foundations and
  roads. These feed both the "can I farm here" and "can I build here"
  questions.

pH back-transformation (VERIFIED, do not change without evidence):
  iSDA transforms most properties, but for pH the file simply stores pH x 10.
  Raw values run 43..254, mean 70.4, i.e. mean pH 7.04, correct for Kenya.
  An earlier assumption of exp(x/10)-1 gave a mean of 7.6 million, which is
  how we caught it. We also STORE pH x 10, as Int16 carrying a 0.1 scale tag,
  so the numbers pass straight through unchanged.

Impossible values are REJECTED, not squashed:
  A handful of edge pixels sit just under the 255 nodata marker and would
  back-transform to nonsense such as pH 25.4. We mark those nodata. Clipping
  them to 10.0 would turn nonsense into a plausible-looking wrong number, and
  a buyer can live with a gap but not with a confident wrong answer.

How to run (from 03_etl with venv active):
  python etl_17_isda_soils.py            # both layers
  python etl_17_isda_soils.py ph         # just pH
  python etl_17_isda_soils.py texture    # just texture
  python etl_17_isda_soils.py ph refetch # force a fresh download of stage A
============================================================================
"""

import os
import sys
import time
import hashlib
from pathlib import Path

# --- Fix PROJ/GDAL database clash with the system PostgreSQL/PostGIS ---------
# PostgreSQL sets PROJ_LIB / GDAL_DATA (system-wide) to its own OLDER copies,
# which breaks rasterio's newer PROJ (error: proj.db LAYOUT.VERSION too old).
# Drop those inherited vars BEFORE importing rasterio so it uses its own bundled
# up-to-date databases. Only affects this process; does not touch PostgreSQL.
for _k in ("PROJ_LIB", "PROJ_DATA", "GDAL_DATA"):
    os.environ.pop(_k, None)

import numpy as np
from dotenv import load_dotenv
from sqlalchemy import create_engine, text

try:
    import rasterio
    from rasterio.windows import Window, from_bounds
    from rasterio.vrt import WarpedVRT
    from rasterio.enums import Resampling
    from rasterio.warp import transform_bounds
    from rasterio.shutil import copy as rio_copy
except ImportError:
    sys.exit("ERROR: rasterio missing. With venv active: pip install rasterio")

BASE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
PROJECT = BASE.parent
PIPELINE = "etl_17_isda_soils"

COG_DIR = PROJECT / "06_rasters" / "cog" / "soils"
COG_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
TMP_DIR = BASE / "data" / "raw" / "soils"
TMP_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

M_PER_DEG = 111320.0

# Strip heights. Stage A is snapped to whole source tile rows at runtime so a
# strip is one contiguous run of bytes on the server. Stage B is local, so the
# only thing that matters there is memory.
FETCH_ROWS_TARGET = 1024
WARP_ROWS = 512

# Pad Kenya's bounding box outward before fetching, so reprojection has real
# pixels to work with at the very edge instead of running off the end.
PAD_DEG = 0.05

BUCKET = "/vsicurl/https://isdasoil.s3.amazonaws.com/soil_data"

ATTRIB = ("iSDAsoil (c) iSDA Africa, CC-BY-4.0; Hengl et al. 2021, "
          "Sci Rep 11:6130.")

# The two layers we ingest. band 1 = the 0-20 cm layer in each file.
LAYERS = [
    {
        "code": "soils.ph", "variable": "ph",
        "url": f"{BUCKET}/ph/ph.tif", "band": 1,
        "transform": "ph_x10",              # pH = stored / 10
        "encode": "int16x10",               # store pH * 10 (0.1 precision)
        "resampling": "bilinear",           # continuous field -> smooth
        "native_name": "native_ph_3857.tif",
        "out_name": "soils_ph_isda_30m.tif",
        "cat_name": ("Soil pH (H2O) 0-20 cm, Int16 scale 0.1 = pH, from iSDAsoil "
                     "(stored as pH x 10). " + ATTRIB),
        "confidence": 3,
        # iSDA marks empty pixels with 255 but does not always declare it in the
        # file header. We must state it explicitly, otherwise a bilinear warp
        # will average 255 into real soil pixels along coastlines and lake
        # edges and invent pH values that never existed.
        "assume_nodata": 255,
    },
    {
        "code": "soils.texture", "variable": "texture_class",
        "url": f"{BUCKET}/texture_class/texture_class.tif", "band": 1,
        "transform": "none",                # categorical class codes
        "encode": "byte",                   # 1..12, 0 = nodata
        "resampling": "nearest",            # categorical -> never blend classes
        "native_name": "native_texture_3857.tif",
        "out_name": "soils_texture_class_isda_30m.tif",
        "cat_name": ("USDA texture class 0-20 cm (1=Clay,2=Silty Clay,3=Sandy "
                     "Clay,4=Clay Loam,5=Silty Clay Loam,6=Sandy Clay Loam,"
                     "7=Loam,8=Silt Loam,9=Sandy Loam,10=Silt,11=Loamy Sand,"
                     "12=Sand), from iSDAsoil. " + ATTRIB),
        "confidence": 3,
        "assume_nodata": 255,
    },
]

OUT_NODATA_I16 = -32768

# Plausible pH range for real soils. Anything outside becomes nodata.
PH_VALID_MIN, PH_VALID_MAX = 3.0, 10.0


def md5_of(path, chunk=8 * 1024 * 1024):
    h = hashlib.md5()
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(chunk), b""):
            h.update(block)
    return h.hexdigest()


# Bump these when the logic that FILLS a stage changes. A leftover working
# file from an older tag is then refused instead of resumed.
STAGE_A_TAG = "A2"
STAGE_B_TAG = "B2"


def write_progress(p, rows, w, h, tag):
    """Record progress as rows plus a fingerprint of what produced them."""
    p.write_text(f"{rows}|{w}|{h}|{tag}")


def read_progress(p, w, h, tag, legacy_ok=False):
    """How many rows a previous run finished, but ONLY if that run was writing
    the SAME grid with the SAME code. Otherwise 0, meaning start again.

    Why this is stricter than it looks: a bare row count is a promise that the
    rows below it are good, and a half-finished file from a different version
    of this script cannot honour that promise. Resuming onto it silently keeps
    old pixels in a file we would then present as new. Redoing a local stage
    costs minutes; shipping a raster we cannot account for costs trust."""
    try:
        parts = p.read_text().strip().split("|")
    except OSError:
        return 0
    if len(parts) == 1:                       # marker from an older version
        return int(parts[0]) if legacy_ok and parts[0].isdigit() else 0
    if len(parts) < 4:
        return 0
    try:
        rows, pw, ph = int(parts[0]), int(parts[1]), int(parts[2])
    except ValueError:
        return 0
    if pw != w or ph != h or parts[3] != tag:
        print(f"    ignoring a stale progress marker ({pw}x{ph} {parts[3]}, "
              f"now {w}x{h} {tag}) and starting this stage fresh.")
        return 0
    return rows


def eta_line(prefix, done, total, t_start, start_row=0):
    """One progress line with speed and a time estimate.

    start_row matters on a resumed run: the clock only started when THIS run
    started, so speed must be measured over the rows this run actually did.
    Dividing by all rows ever written flatters the rate and prints ETA 0."""
    elapsed = time.time() - t_start
    n = done - start_row
    if n <= 0 or elapsed <= 0:
        return
    rate = n / elapsed
    print(f"    {prefix} {done}/{total} rows ({100 * done / total:5.1f}%)  "
          f"{rate * 60:.0f} rows/min  ETA {(total - done) / rate / 60:.0f} min",
          flush=True)


load_dotenv(BASE / ".env")
pw = os.getenv("DB_PASSWORD")
if not pw or pw == "put_your_password_here":
    sys.exit("ERROR: edit the .env file and set DB_PASSWORD first.")

url = (
    f"postgresql+psycopg2://{os.getenv('DB_USER', 'postgres')}:{pw}"
    f"@{os.getenv('DB_HOST', 'localhost')}:{os.getenv('DB_PORT', '5432')}"
    f"/{os.getenv('DB_NAME', 'land_intelligence_kenya')}"
)
engine = create_engine(url)
with engine.connect() as conn:
    conn.execute(text("SELECT 1"))
print("Connected to database:", os.getenv("DB_NAME", "land_intelligence_kenya"))

with engine.begin() as conn:
    run_id = conn.execute(text("""
        INSERT INTO metadata.etl_runs (pipeline, started_at, run_status)
        VALUES (:p, now(), 'running') RETURNING run_id
    """), {"p": PIPELINE}).scalar()
print(f"ETL run opened (run_id={run_id})")

# GDAL settings for remote COG reading.
#   MULTIRANGE=NO is deliberate and hard-won. YES with merged ranges made GDAL
#   fetch huge contiguous byte ranges and effectively stall for hours. NO is
#   the setting that actually completes.
GDAL_ENV = dict(
    GDAL_DISABLE_READDIR_ON_OPEN="EMPTY_DIR",
    CPL_VSIL_CURL_ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS=".tif",
    GDAL_HTTP_MULTIRANGE="NO",
    GDAL_HTTP_MAX_RETRY="10",
    GDAL_HTTP_RETRY_DELAY="2",
    GDAL_HTTP_TIMEOUT="120",
    GDAL_CACHEMAX=1024,                     # MB of block cache (must be int)
    CPL_VSIL_CURL_CACHE_SIZE=536870912,     # 512 MB remote-byte cache
    VSI_CACHE="TRUE",
    VSI_CACHE_SIZE=134217728,
)


# ===========================================================================
# STAGE A - FETCH Kenya's window in the source's OWN grid, no reprojection
# ===========================================================================
def fetch_native(cfg, xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax, refetch=False):
    """Download Kenya's window from the remote Africa COG WITHOUT warping it,
    into a local EPSG:3857 GeoTIFF. Returns (path, nodata_value).

    This is the whole point of version 2. We ask for pixels exactly as the
    server stores them, so each strip is one contiguous run of bytes."""
    native = TMP_DIR / cfg["native_name"]
    progress_file = native.with_suffix(".progress")

    if refetch:
        native.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        progress_file.unlink(missing_ok=True)

    print(f"\n--- STAGE A: fetch native window  <-  {cfg['url']}")

    with rasterio.open(cfg["url"]) as src:
        nodata = src.nodata if src.nodata is not None else cfg.get("assume_nodata")
        bh = src.block_shapes[0][0] or 512
        print(f"    source: {src.count} bands, dtype {src.dtypes[0]}, "
              f"declared nodata {src.nodata}, using nodata {nodata}, "
              f"size {src.width} x {src.height}, block rows {bh}")

        # WHICH GRID IS THIS FILE ACTUALLY IN?
        # We cannot ask PROJ, because iSDA writes its CRS as a LOCAL_CS that
        # PROJ refuses to interpret. But the geotransform is still readable,
        # and it settles the question on its own: measure how wide the file is
        # in its own units. Africa is about 80 degrees wide but about 9 MILLION
        # metres wide, so the two cases are not remotely close and there is no
        # guesswork. We check rather than assume, because getting this wrong
        # would silently fetch the wrong patch of the planet.
        span_x = abs(src.transform.a) * src.width
        native_crs = "EPSG:4326" if span_x < 400 else "EPSG:3857"
        print(f"    file spans {span_x:,.0f} units across -> treating the "
              f"native grid as {native_crs}")

        # Kenya's bbox, converted from lon/lat into the source's own grid.
        # densify_pts follows the curve of the projection so we do not clip a
        # corner.
        x0, y0, x1, y1 = transform_bounds(
            "EPSG:4326", native_crs,
            xmin - PAD_DEG, ymin - PAD_DEG, xmax + PAD_DEG, ymax + PAD_DEG,
            densify_pts=21)

        win = from_bounds(x0, y0, x1, y1, src.transform)
        win = win.round_offsets().round_lengths()
        col_off = max(0, int(win.col_off))
        row_off = max(0, int(win.row_off))
        w = min(int(win.width), src.width - col_off)
        h = min(int(win.height), src.height - row_off)
        if w <= 0 or h <= 0:
            raise SystemExit(f"Empty Kenya window for {cfg['code']} "
                             f"(source {src.width}x{src.height}).")

        transform = src.window_transform(Window(col_off, row_off, w, h))
        px_m = (transform.a if native_crs == "EPSG:3857"
                else transform.a * M_PER_DEG)
        print(f"    Kenya window in native grid: {w} x {h} px "
              f"(~{px_m:.1f} m per pixel), "
              f"{w * h / 1e6:.0f} million pixels")

        # Snap the strip height to whole source tile rows, so one strip maps to
        # one unbroken band of tiles on the server. Then round up to a multiple
        # of 512, which is our OUTPUT block height: writing whole blocks keeps
        # the compressed local file valid when a resumed run reopens it.
        strip = max(bh, (FETCH_ROWS_TARGET // bh) * bh)
        if strip % 512:
            strip = ((strip // 512) + 1) * 512

        # legacy_ok=True: a bare row count written by the first version of this
        # stage is still trustworthy, because the fetch geometry has not
        # changed. This spares an already-downloaded window a 50 minute redo.
        done_rows = (read_progress(progress_file, w, h, STAGE_A_TAG,
                                   legacy_ok=True) if native.exists() else 0)
        if done_rows >= h:
            print(f"    already complete ({h} rows). Skipping download.")
            return native, nodata
        if done_rows:
            print(f"    RESUMING at row {done_rows} of {h}.")

        prof = {
            "driver": "GTiff", "dtype": src.dtypes[0], "count": 1,
            "crs": native_crs, "transform": transform,
            "width": w, "height": h, "nodata": nodata,
            "tiled": True, "blockxsize": 512, "blockysize": 512,
            "compress": "DEFLATE", "bigtiff": "YES",
        }
        mode = "r+" if done_rows > 0 and native.exists() else "w"
        with rasterio.open(native, mode, **({} if mode == "r+" else prof)) as dst:
            t_start = time.time()
            for r in range(done_rows, h, strip):
                sh = min(strip, h - r)
                sw = Window(col_off, row_off + r, w, sh)

                # Retry with a FRESH connection. A dropped tile can leave the
                # HTTP handle stale, so reusing it just fails again.
                arr = None
                for attempt in range(1, 13):
                    try:
                        if attempt == 1:
                            arr = src.read(cfg["band"], window=sw)
                        else:
                            with rasterio.open(cfg["url"]) as s2:
                                arr = s2.read(cfg["band"], window=sw)
                        break
                    except rasterio.errors.RasterioIOError:
                        if attempt == 12:
                            raise
                        print(f"    ... read hiccup at rows {r}-{r + sh}, "
                              f"retry {attempt} (fresh connection)", flush=True)
                        time.sleep(min(3 * attempt, 20))

                dst.write(arr, 1, window=Window(0, r, w, sh))
                write_progress(progress_file, r + sh, w, h, STAGE_A_TAG)
                eta_line("fetch", r + sh, h, t_start, done_rows)

    size_mb = native.stat().st_size / 1_000_000
    print(f"    native window saved: {native.name} ({size_mb:.0f} MB)")
    return native, nodata


# ===========================================================================
# STAGE B - REPROJECT the LOCAL file to EPSG:4326 and transform the values
# ===========================================================================
def warp_and_encode(cfg, native, src_nodata, xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax):
    """Reproject the local native-grid file to lon/lat, convert the values into
    real units, encode them compactly, and write a working GeoTIFF.
    Returns (tmp_path, out_dtype, out_nodata, scale, out_transform)."""
    tmp = TMP_DIR / ("_" + cfg["out_name"])
    progress_file = tmp.with_suffix(".progress")
    print(f"\n--- STAGE B: reproject locally to EPSG:4326 + transform values")

    resamp = (Resampling.bilinear if cfg.get("resampling") == "bilinear"
              else Resampling.nearest)
    if cfg["encode"] == "int16x10":
        out_dtype, out_nodata, scale = "int16", OUT_NODATA_I16, 0.1
    else:
        out_dtype, out_nodata, scale = "uint8", 0, None

    raw_min, raw_max, raw_sum, raw_n = np.inf, -np.inf, 0.0, 0

    with rasterio.open(native) as src:
        with WarpedVRT(src, crs="EPSG:4326", resampling=resamp,
                       src_nodata=src_nodata, nodata=src_nodata) as vrt:
            win = from_bounds(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax, vrt.transform)
            win = win.round_offsets().round_lengths()
            col_off = max(0, int(win.col_off))
            row_off = max(0, int(win.row_off))
            w = min(int(win.width), vrt.width - col_off)
            h = min(int(win.height), vrt.height - row_off)
            if w <= 0 or h <= 0:
                raise SystemExit(f"Empty warped window for {cfg['code']}.")

            out_transform = vrt.window_transform(Window(col_off, row_off, w, h))
            px_deg = vrt.transform.a
            print(f"    output grid: {w} x {h} px "
                  f"(~{px_deg * M_PER_DEG:.1f} m)")

            # legacy_ok=False on purpose. Stage B is local and cheap to redo,
            # so we never inherit rows we cannot account for.
            done_rows = (read_progress(progress_file, w, h, STAGE_B_TAG)
                         if tmp.exists() else 0)
            if done_rows >= h:
                print(f"    already complete ({h} rows). Skipping.")
                return tmp, out_dtype, out_nodata, scale, out_transform, px_deg
            if done_rows:
                print(f"    RESUMING at row {done_rows} of {h}.")

            prof = {
                "driver": "GTiff", "dtype": out_dtype, "count": 1,
                "crs": "EPSG:4326", "transform": out_transform,
                "width": w, "height": h, "nodata": out_nodata,
                "tiled": True, "blockxsize": 512, "blockysize": 512,
                # Compress the working file too. Uncompressed Int16 over
                # Kenya at 30 m is roughly 3 GB of scratch; this cuts it hard
                # and costs very little time. Safe to resume because we only
                # ever write forward, in whole 512-row blocks.
                "compress": "DEFLATE", "predictor": 2,
                "bigtiff": "YES",
            }
            mode = "r+" if done_rows > 0 and tmp.exists() else "w"
            with rasterio.open(tmp, mode, **({} if mode == "r+" else prof)) as dst:
                if scale is not None and mode == "w":
                    dst.scales = (scale,)
                t_start = time.time()
                for r in range(done_rows, h, WARP_ROWS):
                    sh = min(WARP_ROWS, h - r)
                    arr = vrt.read(cfg["band"],
                                   window=Window(col_off, row_off + r, w, sh))

                    good = np.ones(arr.shape, dtype=bool)
                    if src_nodata is not None:
                        good &= (arr != src_nodata)

                    if cfg["transform"] == "ph_x10":
                        # Track the RAW numbers too. Printing raw alongside
                        # transformed is what caught the wrong formula before.
                        if good.any():
                            rv = arr[good]
                            raw_min = min(raw_min, float(rv.min()))
                            raw_max = max(raw_max, float(rv.max()))
                            raw_sum += float(rv.sum()); raw_n += rv.size
                        val = arr.astype("float32")
                        val /= 10.0                       # -> conventional pH
                        # REJECT impossible pH, do not squash it into range.
                        good &= (val >= PH_VALID_MIN) & (val <= PH_VALID_MAX)
                        out = np.full(arr.shape, OUT_NODATA_I16, dtype="int16")
                        out[good] = np.rint(val[good] * 10).astype("int16")
                        del val
                    else:                                  # categorical -> byte
                        out = np.zeros(arr.shape, dtype="uint8")
                        out[good] = arr[good].astype("uint8")

                    dst.write(out, 1, window=Window(0, r, w, sh))
                    write_progress(progress_file, r + sh, w, h, STAGE_B_TAG)
                    eta_line("warp ", r + sh, h, t_start, done_rows)
                    del arr, good, out

    if raw_n:
        cover = ("whole layer" if done_rows == 0
                 else f"rows {done_rows}-{h} only, this run resumed")
        print(f"    RAW source values: min {raw_min:.0f}  max {raw_max:.0f}  "
              f"mean {raw_sum / raw_n:.1f}   (iSDA stores pH x 10; {cover})")
    return tmp, out_dtype, out_nodata, scale, out_transform, px_deg


# ===========================================================================
# STAGE C - sanity check, COG, catalogue
# ===========================================================================
def finalise(cfg, tmp, out_dtype, out_nodata, scale, px_deg):
    out_cog = COG_DIR / cfg["out_name"]

    # Read the stats back from the FINISHED file rather than from the loop, so
    # they are complete even when the run was resumed halfway.
    print("\n--- STAGE C: checking values in the finished file ...")
    s_min, s_max, s_sum, s_n = np.inf, -np.inf, 0.0, 0
    with rasterio.open(tmp) as chk:
        for _, win in chk.block_windows(1):
            blk = chk.read(1, window=win)
            good = blk != out_nodata
            if good.any():
                v = blk[good].astype("float64")
                s_min = min(s_min, float(v.min()))
                s_max = max(s_max, float(v.max()))
                s_sum += float(v.sum()); s_n += v.size
    if s_n:
        f = 0.1 if scale == 0.1 else 1.0        # stored units -> real units
        label = "pH" if cfg["variable"] == "ph" else "class"
        print(f"    {label} values: min {s_min * f:.2f}  max {s_max * f:.2f}  "
              f"mean {(s_sum / s_n) * f:.2f}")
        if cfg["variable"] == "ph":
            print("    EXPECT: min ~4.3, max <= 10.00, mean ~7.0. "
                  "A max above 10 means the reject filter did not run.")
        else:
            print("    EXPECT: min 1, max 12, mean ~5.8.")

    print("    converting to COG ...")
    if out_cog.exists():
        out_cog.unlink()
    rio_copy(str(tmp), str(out_cog), driver="COG",
             compress="DEFLATE", predictor=2,
             overview_resampling="nearest", BIGTIFF="YES")
    tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    tmp.with_suffix(".progress").unlink(missing_ok=True)
    size_mb = out_cog.stat().st_size / 1_000_000
    print(f"    COG: {out_cog.name}  ({size_mb:.0f} MB)")

    with rasterio.open(out_cog) as s:
        bounds = (s.bounds.left, s.bounds.bottom, s.bounds.right, s.bounds.top)
    return out_cog, md5_of(out_cog), round(px_deg * M_PER_DEG, 1), bounds, out_nodata


try:
    with engine.connect() as conn:
        xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax = conn.execute(text("""
            SELECT ST_XMin(e), ST_XMax(e), ST_YMin(e), ST_YMax(e)
            FROM (SELECT ST_Extent(geom) AS e FROM admin.country) t
        """)).one()
        source_id = conn.execute(text(
            "SELECT source_id FROM metadata.sources WHERE name = 'iSDAsoil'"
        )).scalar()
    if source_id is None:
        raise SystemExit("iSDAsoil source missing from metadata. Run etl_04 first.")
    print(f"Kenya bbox: lon {xmin:.3f}..{xmax:.3f}, lat {ymin:.3f}..{ymax:.3f}")

    args = [a.lower() for a in sys.argv[1:]]
    refetch = "refetch" in args
    wanted = [a for a in args if a != "refetch"]
    todo = [c for c in LAYERS
            if not wanted or any(w in c["variable"] or w in c["code"]
                                 for w in wanted)]
    if not todo:
        raise SystemExit(f"No layer matched {wanted}. Use 'ph' and/or 'texture'.")
    print("Layers this run: " + ", ".join(c["code"] for c in todo))
    if refetch:
        print("refetch requested: stage A will download again from scratch.")

    for cfg in todo:
        # Stage A talks to the network, so it runs inside the tuned GDAL env.
        with rasterio.Env(**GDAL_ENV):
            native, src_nodata = fetch_native(cfg, xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax,
                                              refetch=refetch)
        # Stages B and C are purely local. No HTTP settings needed.
        tmp, out_dtype, out_nodata, scale, out_transform, px_deg = \
            warp_and_encode(cfg, native, src_nodata, xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax)
        cog, chk, px_m, bounds, ndv = finalise(cfg, tmp, out_dtype, out_nodata,
                                               scale, px_deg)

        with engine.connect() as conn:
            dataset_id = conn.execute(text(
                "SELECT dataset_id FROM metadata.datasets WHERE code = :c"
            ), {"c": cfg["code"]}).scalar()
        if dataset_id is None:
            raise SystemExit(f"{cfg['code']} dataset missing. Run etl_04.")

        with engine.begin() as conn:
            conn.execute(text("""
                DELETE FROM metadata.raster_catalog
                WHERE dataset_id = :did AND variable = :v
            """), {"did": dataset_id, "v": cfg["variable"]})
            conn.execute(text("""
                INSERT INTO metadata.raster_catalog
                    (dataset_id, name, variable, storage_url, format,
                     pixel_size_m, band_count, nodata_value, temporal_start,
                     temporal_end, bbox, checksum, source_id, source_date,
                     confidence)
                VALUES
                    (:did, :name, :v, :url, 'COG', :px, 1, :nodata,
                     DATE '2001-01-01', DATE '2017-01-01',
                     ST_MakeEnvelope(:l, :b, :r, :t, 4326),
                     :chk, :sid, DATE '2021-01-01', :conf)
            """), {
                "did": dataset_id, "name": cfg["cat_name"], "v": cfg["variable"],
                "url": str(cog), "px": px_m, "nodata": ndv,
                "l": bounds[0], "b": bounds[1], "r": bounds[2], "t": bounds[3],
                "chk": chk, "sid": source_id, "conf": cfg["confidence"],
            })
            conn.execute(text("""
                UPDATE metadata.datasets SET etl_status = 'ingested',
                       updated_at = now() WHERE code = :c
            """), {"c": cfg["code"]})
        print(f"    catalogued {cfg['code']}.")

    with engine.begin() as conn:
        conn.execute(text("""
            UPDATE metadata.etl_runs
            SET finished_at = now(), run_status = 'success', rows_out = :r
            WHERE run_id = :id
        """), {"r": len(todo), "id": run_id})

    print(f"\nDONE. Run {run_id} logged as success.")
    print("The native download in 03_etl/data/raw/soils is kept on purpose: if "
          "we ever change the encoding or the pH rule, stage B can rerun off "
          "the local copy in minutes with no download at all.")
    print("QGIS: add soils_ph (smooth, acidic in the highlands, alkaline in the "
          "ASALs) and soils_texture_class (blocky classes; heavy clay = black "
          "cotton risk).")

except Exception as exc:
    with engine.begin() as conn:
        conn.execute(text("""
            UPDATE metadata.etl_runs
            SET finished_at = now(), run_status = 'failed', error_message = :e
            WHERE run_id = :id
        """), {"e": str(exc)[:2000], "id": run_id})
    raise
